nym/acronym anchor or a gene/enzyme-premodified compound naming the condition.\n\nI should also fold in prepositional-complement reorderings like \"deficiency in [gene]\" so they're labeled the same as the compound form when the paraphrase names a recognized condition, and I need to work out the negative constraints that carve out exceptions to this rule.\n\nThe negative side covers spans that are purely descriptive — measurements of enzyme activity, symptom lists with non-condition heads like fever or rash, bare generic class terms that belong under DiseaseClass, and modifier-only tokens like laterality or severity that don't themselves form a naming unit. I'm now drafting the full principle as a nested markdown bullet list titled around nomenclature-anchored condition labeling, with the IF/THEN rule for when a span counts as a fixed nomenclature unit (eponym/acronym anchor, gene/enzyme-premodified compound or its prepositional reordering, established stock term, or an appositive/parenthetical alternative name), followed by the negative constraints distinguishing descriptive measurements, broad category terms, and bare modifiers from true condition names, plus a closing generalization check that tests form rather than vocabulary. I'm keeping the output as just this single bulleted principle with no preface, resolving the tension between \"stock term\" and \"broad category term\" by clarifying that (c) only covers stock terms for specific conditions, not generic class words.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Nomenclature-Anchored Condition Labeling:** **IF** a span's head noun denotes a condition, including sign, process, or activity/state nouns, **and** the span functions as a fixed nomenclature unit, **THEN** label the span SpecificDisease, whether it appears as a modifier stack, a premodifier compound, or a prepositional complement. A span functions as a nomenclature unit if it:\n  - (a) contains an eponym or acronym that anchors the head;\n  - (b) is a gene-, enzyme-, or substance-premodified compound whose head names a condition, or a reordered prepositional-complement form of that same compound (e.g., \"[substance] deficiency\" / \"deficiency in [substance]\"; \"deficient activity of [enzyme]\") whose compound paraphrase is a recognized condition name;\n  - (c) is an established stock term naming a specific condition;\n  - (d) is introduced as an alternative name for an already-labeled disease through \"so-called,\" an appositive, or a parenthetical restatement.\n  \n  **Negative constraints (does NOT apply when):**\n  - The head is a sign or state noun with no anchor, and its compound paraphrase is not a recognized condition name, so the span only describes a feature, finding, or measured quantity (e.g., activity levels, values, counts).\n  - The span is a broad, unanchored class-level term. Such terms belong to DiseaseClass under the entity definitions and must not be defaulted to SpecificDisease.\n  - Modifier-only tokens (laterality, severity, numeric, temporal) do not form a nomenclature unit with a condition head. They keep the Modifier label.\n  - A manifestation verb or preposition (\"presenting as,\" \"characterized by\") introduces the span. The test applies to the span's own form, not to the introducing frame.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2373, "output_tokens": 4155, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 3556}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}